                <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                  KILZ built its reputation on moisture and stain-blocking primers, and that heritage shows up clearly in this 1-part epoxy acrylic formula: it's the product in this roundup best suited to below-grade concrete that deals with occasional dampness. The formula tolerates slightly higher substrate moisture than most competitors without adhesion failure — a genuinely useful trait for basement floors, which almost never test as perfectly dry the way an above-grade garage slab does. We applied it to a below-grade basement floor with a history of minor seasonal dampness (not standing water, but the kind of humidity migration common in older foundations), and after five months saw no lifting, no bubbling, and no efflorescence pushing through the coating. Application is identical to any other 1-part product: no etching required on a clean, sound floor, a single coat for light use or two coats for a more finished look, and a workable open time that makes it easy for a first-time painter to maintain a wet edge across a large basement floor. Where it falls slightly short of our top pick is color selection — the palette skews toward a handful of grays and one or two accent colors rather than the broader range Rust-Oleum offers — and the cured finish is a touch flatter and less glossy. For a basement, porch, or utility room where the priority is moisture tolerance over showroom shine, this is our first recommendation.
                </p>

