                  Drylok has spent decades as a trusted name in basement waterproofing paint, and the E1 line applies that same "keep water out of concrete" philosophy to a 1-part epoxy floor paint sold at a genuinely budget-friendly price point. It's the least expensive product in this roundup by a meaningful margin, and it doesn't pretend to be anything more than what it is: an accessible, easy-to-apply floor paint with epoxy resin added for extra toughness over Drylok's standard formulas. We tested it in a detached workshop with a history of never having been coated before — bare, dusty, moderately porous concrete typical of an older outbuilding. Coverage per gallon ran slightly lower than the other products in this guide because the porous surface soaked up more of the first coat, which is worth budgeting for if your slab has never been sealed. Once cured, the finish held up acceptably to foot traffic, rolling tool carts, and dropped hand tools over four months of workshop use, with only minor surface scuffing in the immediate area around a workbench. It is noticeably thinner-feeling underfoot than the Rust-Oleum or KILZ products and shows wear sooner under sustained abrasion, but for anyone coating a workshop, shed, or utility room on a tight budget, it does the job without unnecessary frills.
