                  We're including Insl-x Sure Step in this guide as an important exception: it's a straight acrylic anti-slip coating rather than an epoxy-modified formula, and we think that distinction matters enough to call out rather than bury in a spec sheet. Sure Step ships with fine anti-slip aggregate already blended into the can, producing a textured, slip-resistant surface right out of the box without a separate broadcast step — a real advantage for basement stairwells, exterior steps, pool decksides, and utility room floors near sinks or water heaters where slip resistance matters more than raw hardness or chemical resistance. In our testing on a basement stairwell landing that stays damp near an adjacent laundry sink, the anti-slip texture provided a noticeably more secure footing than any of the smoother epoxy-acrylic products in this guide, wet or dry. It is not the product to reach for if your priority is a smooth, glossy showroom finish, and its resistance to petroleum products and heavy abrasion trails behind the true epoxy-acrylic formulas above — this is a safety-focused coating first and a durability play second. For the specific use case of a damp, slip-prone concrete surface where someone could genuinely get hurt on a slick floor, it's the right tool, and we'd choose it over any smoother epoxy paint in this guide for that application specifically.
