        <!-- SECTION 4: Best Uses -->
        <div id="best-uses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Where Epoxy Paint Makes the Most Sense
          </h2>
          <p>
            Because 1-part epoxy paint trades some durability for simplicity and cost, it performs best when matched to the right room. Here's how we'd think about it space by space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Basement Floors</h3>
          <p>
            Basements are arguably the single best use case for epoxy paint. Foot traffic rather than vehicle weight is the dominant stress on the coating, and the lower cost and easier application matter more when you're coating a finished or semi-finished basement rather than a garage. The moisture tolerance question matters more here than anywhere else in the house — if your basement has any history of dampness or vapor transmission through the slab, look specifically for a product (like KILZ, above) with demonstrated performance on borderline-damp concrete, and consider a dedicated concrete moisture test before committing to any coating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Porches and Patios</h3>
          <p>
            Covered porches and patios see foot traffic, furniture weight, and weather exposure, but rarely vehicle contact. Epoxy paint's lower cost per square foot and easy application make it an efficient way to refresh a tired-looking porch floor, and the ability to color-match to your home's exterior palette (a strength of the Behr product above) is often more valuable here than raw durability.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Workshops and Utility Rooms</h3>
          <p>
            Workshops see a different kind of abuse than garages — dropped tools, rolling carts, occasional chemical spills — but typically not the sustained hot-tire contact that wears down epoxy paint fastest. A budget-friendly 1-part product is often the right call here, especially in a detached workshop or shed where the floor has never been coated before.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Light-Use Garages</h3>
          <p>
            If your garage is used for storage, a workbench, or occasional parking rather than daily vehicle use, epoxy paint can work well and save significant money over a full 2-part kit. The line we'd draw: if a vehicle is parked on the same section of floor every single day and driven immediately after (meaning hot tires contact the coating repeatedly), you're in territory where a true epoxy kit will substantially outlast any product on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: When to Upgrade -->
        <div id="when-to-upgrade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When to Step Up to a Real Epoxy Kit
          </h2>
          <p>
            We'd rather talk you out of the wrong purchase than sell you the wrong product. Epoxy paint is the right choice for a large share of concrete-coating projects, but it is the wrong choice for certain situations — and buying it anyway usually means recoating far sooner than you expected.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Step Up to a True 2-Part Epoxy Kit If...</p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 list-none p-0 m-0">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-gray-200"><span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black">→</span> You park a daily-driven vehicle in the same spot every day, especially in a hot climate where tires heat up during driving</li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-gray-200"><span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black">→</span> Your garage sees heavy tool cart traffic, dropped metal parts, or regular contact with motor oil, brake fluid, or de-icing salt</li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-gray-200"><span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black">→</span> You want a coating that genuinely lasts a decade or more without a recoat</li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-gray-200"><span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black">→</span> You want the glossy, thick, professionally finished look of a true epoxy floor with decorative color chips</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>
            If any of those describe your situation, our full <a href="/best-epoxy-garage-floor-kit" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold no-underline hover:underline">Best Epoxy Garage Floor Kit guide</a> covers the true 2-part water-based, high-solids, and polycuramine systems that will substantially outlast anything on this page. It's a bigger project — mixing, pot-life management, proper acid etching — but it's the right bigger project for a floor that takes real daily abuse.
          </p>
          <p>
            On the other hand, if you're coating a basement, porch, utility room, or a garage that mostly sees storage and occasional parking, everything above this section still applies: a 1-part epoxy paint will very likely do the job well, cost a fraction of the price, and take a fraction of the time to apply.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 1-part epoxy paint actually epoxy?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Partially. Most products sold as "1-part epoxy paint" or "epoxy acrylic" are water-based acrylic paints with epoxy resin blended in to improve hardness and chemical resistance — not a pure two-part epoxy system. They dry primarily through water evaporation, like ordinary paint, rather than curing through the chemical cross-linking reaction that makes true 2-part epoxy so hard and durable. That's not a knock on the products — it's simply a different chemistry that trades some durability for dramatically easier application.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to acid-etch the concrete before applying epoxy paint?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Usually not, which is one of the biggest appeals of 1-part epoxy paint. Most manufacturers only require a thorough cleaning and degreasing of the concrete, plus scuff-sanding or a light mechanical profile if the surface is very smooth or previously sealed. This is a meaningfully lower bar than a true 2-part epoxy kit, which almost always requires acid etching for proper adhesion. Always check your specific product's instructions, since surface prep requirements vary between brands and formulas.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does epoxy paint last on a garage or basement floor?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">In our testing and based on manufacturer guidance, expect roughly <strong>2–5 years</strong> before a recoat is needed in light-to-moderate traffic conditions like basements, porches, and light-use garages. Under daily vehicle parking, especially with hot tires, expect the shorter end of that range or less. A true 2-part epoxy kit, by comparison, typically lasts 5–20+ years depending on the specific product and traffic level.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply epoxy paint over old paint or a previous coating?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, in most cases, provided the existing coating is fully intact, clean, and not peeling. Scuff-sand the surface with fine-grit sandpaper to promote adhesion, then clean thoroughly before applying. If the existing coating is peeling or delaminating anywhere, remove it completely in that area first — applying new paint over a failing coating will not fix the underlying adhesion problem, and the new coat will fail in the same spots.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is epoxy paint safe for a basement with occasional moisture?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Some formulas tolerate minor, occasional moisture better than others, but none of the products in this guide are designed for a floor with active water intrusion or standing water. If your basement has ongoing moisture problems, address the source (drainage, sump pump, foundation sealing) before coating the floor with any product. For basements with only mild, seasonal humidity concerns, a moisture-tolerant formula like KILZ's 1-part epoxy acrylic performed well in our testing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

